<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/foundational/free-will-and-determinism">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every time you choose tea over coffee, or stay a little longer at a friend's house, you feel
    that you <em>could have done otherwise</em>. That feeling seems obvious — and yet it collides
    with one of the most successful ideas in human history: that the universe runs on laws. If
    every event follows inevitably from earlier events, and if your brain is a physical thing
    inside that universe, then your "choice" was the output of causes that were already in place
    before you were born. The feeling of freedom would then be something like the feeling the
    Sun has of circling the Earth: vivid, universal, and wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    This collision is what philosophers call the problem of <strong>free will and
    determinism</strong>, and it matters far beyond the seminar room. Courts punish people
    because they assume the punished <em>could have obeyed the law</em>. Parents praise children,
    employers reward workers, friends forgive friends — all of this talk of desert seems to
    presuppose that people are the true origins of their actions. If determinism is true, that
    presupposition is under threat, and an entire legal and moral order built on it has to be
    rethought. So philosophers have mapped the positions with unusual care, and the map is the
    subject of this chapter.
  </p>
  <p>
    The three great positions: <strong>hard determinism</strong> (determinism is true, so free
    will is an illusion), <strong>libertarianism</strong> (free will is real, so determinism must
    be false, at least in the right places), and <strong>compatibilism</strong> (free will and
    determinism never actually conflicted — freedom means something else entirely). The widget
    below walks through the logic of each in turn.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the threat, the denials, and the dominant compromise" widget="equationStep" config={fwCfg} />

  <h2>Determinism: the threat, stated precisely</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Determinism</strong> is the thesis that, given the complete state of the world at one
    time and the laws of nature, only one future is possible. Notice what it does <em>not</em>
    say. It does not say your choices don't matter, or that nothing you do makes a difference —
    your deliberations are part of the causal chain, and they may be exactly what produces the
    outcome. It says your deliberations themselves have causes, going back before you existed.
    Spinoza put the diagnosis beautifully in the 1670s: men believe themselves free
    <em>"because they are conscious of their volitions and ignorant of the causes by which those
    volitions are determined"</em> — we feel free because we can't see our own gears.
  </p>
  <p>
    A common first response is to invoke quantum mechanics: physics, since the 1920s, describes
    outcomes at the smallest scales as genuinely probabilistic, so maybe the future is not fixed
    after all. But this rescue fails on inspection. If your "choice" is the result of a quantum
    dice-roll inside a synapse, the roll is <em>random</em> — and a random choice is no more
    yours than a determined one. Randomness is not control. Imagine a shuffling machine that
    sometimes injects pure noise into your decisions: that is not freedom, it is a slot machine
    in the skull. This is the standard dilemma facing any attempt to locate free will in physics:
    <strong>determined</strong> feels unfree, <strong>random</strong> feels unfree, and every
    event is some combination of the two. That dilemma is the engine of the whole debate.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is also <em>theological</em> determinism to consider, and it produced the debate's
    oldest form. If God is <strong>omniscient</strong> — knows everything, including the future —
    then God already knows what you will do tomorrow. But then, it seems, you cannot do anything
    else: if you could, God's knowledge would be wrong, which is impossible. The medieval answer
    (Boethius, sixth century) is that God is <em>timeless</em>, outside the flow of time
    altogether: God doesn't "foresee" your choice, God simply sees it, as you see a bird crossing
    the sky, and watching does not force the bird. A God <em>everlasting</em> — existing within
    time, moment by moment — has a harder time with the argument. This is the problem of the
    compatibility of omniscience and human freedom, and it is structurally identical to the
    scientific one: perfect prediction replaces perfect laws, and the question is the same.
  </p>

  <h2>Hard determinism: accept the consequence</h2>
  <p>
    The most direct response is to accept determinism and deny free will. <strong>Hard
    determinists</strong> such as Spinoza, Baron d'Holbach and, in our own time, Derk Pereboom,
    argue that the popular conception of freedom — that you could have done otherwise in exactly
    the same circumstances, that you are the ultimate origin of your actions — is simply false,
    and that we should live honestly with that. Pereboom's phrase for the resulting life is
    "living without free will": we would still imprison people, but for <em>protection,
    deterrence and rehabilitation</em>, not retribution. Punishment as "deserved suffering"
    would be abandoned, the way public flogging was abandoned.
  </p>
  <p>
    The view has an uncomfortable cost that its opponents press hard: ordinary moral life seems
    to collapse. When a friend keeps a promise, praise seems appropriate; when someone betrays
    you, anger seems natural. The hard determinist must say these reactions are, in some deep
    sense, misfiring — or at least that their "desert" version should be dropped. Some
    philosophers (P. F. Strawson, in his 1962 essay <em>Freedom and Resentment</em>) replied that
    this is psychologically impossible: reactive emotions like gratitude and resentment are not
    optional equipment we can put down once we believe a theory. Whether hard determinism is
    livable is itself a live philosophical question.
  </p>

  <h2>Libertarian free will: break the chain</h2>
  <p>
    The opposite response accepts that free will exists and concludes that determinism must be
    false — at least where human agents are concerned. <strong>Libertarians</strong> (in this
    technical sense — nothing to do with the political position) argue that when a person makes a
    genuinely free choice, that event is <em>not determined</em> by prior causes, yet is not
    random either: it is caused by the <em>agent</em> herself, a special kind of causation.
    Roderick Chisholm called this "agent causation" — the person, not an event, is the cause.
  </p>
  <p>
    The immediate problem is the one already met: how does this dodge the dilemma? If the choice
    is undetermined, isn't it just random with better branding? Libertarians answer that the
    agent <em>controls</em> the choice in a way no physical process can — you settle it. Critics
    answer that this is a name for the mystery, not a solution to it, and that it sits poorly
    with neuroscience, where mental events track brain events with increasing precision. Still,
    libertarianism has formidable defenders (Robert Kane argues that randomness in the brain's
    decision-making can be <em>amplified and settled by</em> an agent's own reasons and efforts,
    giving undetermined but self-controlled choice), and it preserves the strongest reading of
    "could have done otherwise".
  </p>

  <h2>The consequence argument: the strongest card of the incompatibilist</h2>
  <p>
    Why do so many philosophers think determinism rules out the popular freedom? Because of a
    short, sharp argument published by Peter van Inwagen in 1983, called the <strong>consequence
    argument</strong>. It runs: (1) Nobody has power over the laws of nature. (2) Nobody has
    power over the state of the world in the distant past — including the state before they were
    born. (3) But if determinism is true, our present actions are an inevitable consequence of
    those two things, the laws plus the past. (4) If you have no power over X, and no power over
    Y, and X and Y jointly entail Z, you have no power over Z. So: nobody has power over their
    own present actions.
  </p>
  <p>
    A vivid way to see the point: imagine jumping into a time machine and arriving in the year
    1500 — you cannot affect the past you find there. But if determinism is true, your actions
    <em>today</em> are as fixed a consequence of 1500 as tomorrow's sunrise. Your choices are,
    in van Inwagen's phrase, "a consequence of the laws of nature and events in the remote past",
    and that consequence is not up to you. The argument's premises feel almost undeniable — which
    is why the compatibilist's escape route is not to deny them but to argue that the conclusion,
    however true, does not mean what it seems to mean.
  </p>

  <h2>Compatibilism: redefining the freedom that matters</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Compatibilism</strong> — associated with Thomas Hobbes and David Hume in the
    eighteenth century and dominant today (about 59% of professional philosophers in the 2020
    PhilPapers survey) — agrees with the hard determinist that determinism is true, and with the
    libertarian that free will exists. Its move is radical: the two were never in conflict,
    because "free will" never meant <em>escaping causation</em>. It meant something entirely
    available inside a determined world.
  </p>
  <p>
    The classical Humean version: an action is free when it flows from your own desires and
    decisions, <em>without external constraint</em>. A prisoner free to walk about his cell has
    compatibilist freedom within it; when chained, he doesn't. Notice this is a distinction that
    determinism cannot erase — between the determined action of someone acting on their own
    reasons, and the determined action of someone coerced at gunpoint, there is all the
    difference in the world, and it is the difference we actually care about.
  </p>
  <p>
    Harry Frankfurt sharpened this in 1971 with <strong>second-order desires</strong>: you are
    free when you want your wants. An addict wants the drug but does not want to want it — his
    will is divided, and he is unfree. A person whose first-order desires line up with what she
    endorses on reflection has the only freedom worth wanting. Frankfurt then went further in
    1969 and attacked the deep assumption behind the consequence argument: the "principle of
    alternate possibilities", that you are responsible only if you could have done otherwise.
    Consider a neuroscientist with a device that will force you to vote for a certain candidate
    if you show any sign of voting otherwise — but you vote that way entirely on your own, and
    the device never activates. You could not have done otherwise (the device was waiting), yet
    you acted freely and are fully responsible. Alternate possibilities, Frankfurt argued, are
    not what freedom is made of; <em>responsiveness to reasons</em> is. Compatibilists like
    Daniel Dennett built on this: freedom worth having is the capacity to respond to reasons,
    foresee consequences and act on your own evaluations — abilities that exist, and exist
    more or less of, in a determined world, and that a random quantum dice-roll would only spoil.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> compatibilism is not a cowardly fudge that "redefines the
  problem away". It is a substantive claim that the freedom presupposed by law and morality —
  acting on your own reasons without coercion — is real, well-defined, and unaffected by
  determinism. The dispute with the incompatibilist is about which concept deserves the name
  "free will", and that dispute is fought with arguments about desert, manipulation and the
  foundations of blame, not with dictionary tricks.</p>

  <h2>Why the answer has consequences</h2>
  <p>
    The stakes show up immediately in criminal law. <strong>Retributive</strong> theories of
    punishment — the person deserves to suffer because they chose wrongly — presuppose deep
    freedom. If that presupposition fails, punishment can only be justified
    <em>consequentially</em>: deterrence, incapacitation, rehabilitation. Notice that our actual
    law already carves out excuses that look exactly like the compatibilist's criteria: insanity,
    coercion, infancy and duress excuse because the agent was not properly responding to
    reasons. The law, arguably, is already compatibilist.
  </p>
  <p>
    The stakes also show up in the lab. <strong>Benjamin Libet's experiments</strong> (1983) are
    routinely cited as disproving free will: participants watching a fast clock reported deciding
    to move at about 200 ms, but a "readiness potential" — a build-up of brain activity —
    preceded their report by over half a second. Headlines announced that the brain decides
    before the conscious mind does. But the interpretation is fiercely contested: the readiness
    potential may be background noise rather than the start of a decision (later work by Aaron
    Schurger suggests it reflects fluctuating neural activity that crosses a threshold), the
    reported time is a retrospective judgement with its own errors, and spontaneous finger
    flexions in a lab are a thin proxy for deliberative, reasons-governed choice. Libet himself
    proposed a "free won't" — conscious veto — to rescue some role for the agent. The experiments
    constrain certain pictures of the mind; they settle nothing about the argument between
    determinist and compatibilist.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Manipulation cases are the compatibilist's hardest test. Imagine a neuroscientist who
    <em>implants</em> your desire — engineers your values so that you then act on them
    "freely", by Frankfurt's own criteria, with no coercion at all. Intuitively you are not
    responsible, yet determinism says your desires were likewise implanted — by heredity and
    upbringing. This is the <strong>manipulation argument</strong>, and compatibilists have
    answered in two ways: by finding a principled difference between machine implantation and
    ordinary causation of character (Dennett argues there is none that matters, so our intuition
    misfires), or by adding a "history" condition to freedom — desires must be acquired through
    reasonably responsive processes. The debate is unresolved and is where the live action is.
  </p>
  <p>
    A second frontier is the <strong>hard-incompatibilist</strong> position of Pereboom: even if
    determinism is false (say quantum indeterminacy is real), the randomness in our neural
    machinery still rules out ultimate desert — so neither determinism nor indeterminism secures
    the strong freedom. Against him, compatibilists reply that "ultimate" origination may be an
    incoherent demand, like asking an author to write a book that was not written out of
    anything. And a third strand is empirical: studies by Vohs and Schooler found that reading
    anti-free-will passages made participants cheat more — suggesting belief in free will
    itself does moral work, whatever the metaphysical truth. The 150-year-old verdict of
    William James — that the problem is a "quandary" every serious mind must face — still
    stands; but the majority view has shifted to a subtle one: the universe may be a machine,
    and we may still be responsible inside it.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Frankfurt, "Alternate Possibilities and Moral Responsibility" (1969) and "Freedom of the Will and the Concept of a Person" (1971); van Inwagen, An Essay on Free Will (1983); Dennett, Freedom Evolves (2003); Pereboom, Living Without Free Will (2001); Strawson, "Freedom and Resentment" (1962); Libet et al., "Time of Conscious Intention to Act" (1983); Schurger et al., PNAS (2012); 2020 PhilPapers Survey.</p>
